<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.reine.fomocapture  |  BUILD STEP 6, PLAN.md decision 6.

  Runs the FOMO leaderboard capture once a day. This file lives in fomo/ and is
  SYMLINKED into ~/Library/LaunchAgents/, so the scheduling is version
  controlled alongside the thing it schedules.

  It launches run_capture.sh and nothing else. Every path here is absolute,
  because a launch agent inherits no shell startup file and no useful PATH.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.reine.fomocapture</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/reinestarr/Trading with Claude Code/fomo/run_capture.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>/Users/reinestarr/Trading with Claude Code/fomo</string>

  <!--
    TWO LOG FILES, and pointing these two keys at the OTHER one is the fix for
    a real defect. They used to name logs/capture.log, which is the file the
    python's own logger writes -- and because that logger also echoes to
    stdout, every line it wrote landed in that file TWICE. Twenty-two captures
    produced forty-four "manifest row appended" lines, which is exactly the
    kind of doubling that makes a person mistrust a count.

    So they are split by who writes them, and under launchd the split is now
    clean -- no line appears in both:

      logs/capture.log  -- the python's own logger, written directly by a
                           FileHandler. One line per event. This is the run's
                           story: which mode the live-capture interlock was
                           in, what was captured, what was skipped, what
                           failed and why. START HERE.
      logs/launchd.log  -- everything launchd sees on the job's stdout and
                           stderr, and ONLY the things capture.log cannot
                           carry: run_capture.sh's own banner lines, a
                           traceback from an import that died before logging
                           was configured, and Playwright's and Chrome's own
                           stderr chatter.

    THE ECHO IS CONDITIONAL, which is what makes the split clean. The python
    adds a stderr handler only when sys.stdout.isatty() -- so a person running
    ./run_capture.sh by hand still watches it work in the terminal, while
    under launchd stdout is this file, isatty() is False, and no copy is made.
    Measured before that change: 226 of 226 lines in launchd.log were
    byte-identical duplicates of capture.log lines. See setup_logging().

    launchd APPENDS to both, so each accumulates fire after fire in order. If
    the python never starts at all, the wrapper's two banner lines in
    launchd.log are the only evidence the job fired -- that is the case this
    redirect exists for, and capture.log would be silent.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/reinestarr/Trading with Claude Code/fomo/logs/launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/reinestarr/Trading with Claude Code/fomo/logs/launchd.log</string>

  <!--
    07:30 local AND RunAtLoad, both, because macOS is not a server: it does
    not run jobs while asleep and it does not catch up a fire it slept
    through. StartCalendarInterval alone would silently miss every day the lid
    was shut at 07:30. RunAtLoad covers login, wake and unlock.

    Firing three ways is only safe because the capture is idempotent per UTC
    date: anything already OK today is skipped, writes nothing and costs
    nothing. See the IDEMPOTENCE block in capture_leaderboard.py.
  -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <array>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>7</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>

  <!--
    Standard, NOT Background. ProcessType is a scheduling contract, and
    Background means "throttle this thing's CPU and I/O; nobody is waiting".
    That is wrong for a five and a half minute headed browser session: it
    would stretch page loads, push renders past the readiness timeouts this
    script measured, and turn slow boards into FAIL rows carrying a
    zero-anchor error that says nothing about the real cause.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Standard</string>

  <!--
    launchd must NOT treat the browser as its own child to clean up. Playwright
    spawns Chrome detached, into its own session, and a capture run is a tree
    of processes rather than one. With this false, launchd can reap that tree
    out from under a run that is part way through a board. Teardown is the
    wrapper's job instead: run_capture.sh walks the parent and child chain
    itself and kills it thoroughly, on timeout and on SIGTERM.
  -->
  <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key><true/>

  <!--
    THE BOARD DOES NOT RENDER HEADLESS. Measured twice: headless renders the
    page shell and the leaderboard XHR never fires, so there is nothing to
    capture. That is settled (PLAN.md, RESOLVED BY MEASUREMENT 2, and
    launch_browser's docstring); HEADLESS stays False and nothing here should
    try to work around it.

    So this job REQUIRES a real logged in GUI session, and Aqua is how that
    requirement is stated to launchd. Loaded into a background or pre-login
    session type there is no window server to draw into, and the failure is
    not a clean refusal: it is a run that produces 22 FAIL rows for a reason
    no column explains.
  -->
  <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key><string>Aqua</string>

  <!--
    An explicit, absolute PATH. A launch agent's inherited PATH is minimal and
    is not what a login shell has. run_capture.sh names every binary it uses
    absolutely, so this is not what the wrapper depends on; it is what
    anything FURTHER down depends on, including Playwright's node driver and
    Chrome itself. Four system directories, nothing added: the venv python,
    the driver and the browser are all invoked by absolute path, so there is
    nothing to gain by widening this and a version skew to lose.
  -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>

  <!--
    ExitTimeOut is how long launchd waits after SIGTERM before SIGKILL when it
    stops this job. The default of 20s is too short here: on SIGTERM the
    wrapper kills the whole capture tree, and that takes the 10s grace period
    it gives Chrome to shut down cleanly plus the SIGKILL pass afterwards. 90s
    is that teardown with room to spare on a loaded machine. Too short and
    launchd SIGKILLs the wrapper mid teardown, which is precisely how a headed
    Chrome gets abandoned on screen.
  -->
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key><integer>90</integer>

  <!--
    The floor under how often this job may be RELAUNCHED. launchd defaults to
    10s. 300s here because RunAtLoad means every bootout and bootstrap cycle,
    and every wake, fires the job again.

    WHAT THE CHEAP PATH ACTUALLY IS, stated as the code now behaves rather
    than as it was once hoped to. A run whose boards are all already captured
    for today reads captures.csv, stats the raw files those rows name, finds
    nothing to do and exits 0 WITHOUT LAUNCHING A BROWSER -- milliseconds of
    work, no window on screen. That is preflight_skip() in
    capture_leaderboard.py, and it is what makes firing on every wake
    affordable.

    IT IS NOT THE ONLY PATH, and 300s does not bound the other one. Within one
    capture's worth of time of UTC midnight (MAX_CAPTURE_SECONDS, about six
    minutes), the pre-flight deliberately refuses to guess which date a
    capture would land on, so the run falls through to a full browser session
    -- 22 page loads, about 332 seconds measured -- before it can decide to
    write nothing. The same is true of any run that genuinely has a board to
    capture. ThrottleInterval does not and cannot cap that; what caps it is
    that launchd will not start a second copy of a label that is already
    running, and that capture_leaderboard.py takes a run-wide lock so a
    by-hand run overlapping a launchd run stands down instead of duplicating.

    So what this value buys is narrow and worth stating narrowly: a hard floor
    on how often a WAKE STORM can start the job at all. It cannot delay the
    real work -- five minutes is nothing against a 24h cadence. It IS visible
    when testing by hand: repeated `launchctl kickstart` calls inside five
    minutes will be held off, and that is the intended behaviour, not a fault.
  -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>300</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
